{"step_index":754,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:34:12Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Roman Weight and Radius**\n\nConsidering the case where the Roman weight of *f* equals 2, if there's only one vertex with value '2' and others are zero, it must be adjacent to all other vertices. This implies the graph's radius is at most 1. Consequently, if the Roman weight is 2, the radius plus 1 is less than or equal to the Roman weight.\n\nIf the graph's radius is at least 2, the Roman weight must be at least 3. Now, if radius is exactly 2, the radius plus one, being 3, is also less than or equal to ...\n\n\n**Refining Roman Weight and Radius Bounds**\n\nIf the graph radius is at least 3, then a Roman weight of 3 is not possible. Considering all possibilities, any Roman dominating function with a Roman weight of 3 would force the graph radius to be less than or equal to 2, therefore, to have a radius greater than or equal to 3 the Roman weight needs to be at least 4. Attempting a contrapositive, if the Roman weight is 2, the radius is less than or equal to 1, consequently radius + 1 is less or equal to Roman weight, by contradiction.\n\n\n**Exploring Roman Weight and Radius Relationships**\n\nExamining the case of a Roman weight equal to 2, it is impossible to have two vertices with a value of 1 because other vertices would have a value of 0 and this is not a Roman dominating function, if |V| > 2, hence, it reduces to the case of a single vertex with $f(u) = 2$ requiring all others to be adjacent to $u$. This implies that the radius is at most 1, thus radius + 1 ≤ Roman weight for weights <= 2.\n\nNow, considering a Roman weight of 3, the graph must have three vertices, since any graph with more than three vertices would need a 2 value to be Roman dominated. Connecte